## Methods
Synthesis of $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobdc) nanocrystals. All nanocrystal syntheses were conducted at room temperature. Solid 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid $(1.0\mathrm{g},5.0\mathrm{mmol}$ ; $\mathrm{H}_{4}(\mathrm{dobdc}))$ and $16\mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ , Co, Ni) or $\mathrm{{MnCl}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{4H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were added to a mixture of $400\mathrm{ml}$ of dimethylformamide (DMF), $27\mathrm{ml}$ of ethanol, and $27\mathrm{ml}$ of water in a ${500}\mathrm{-ml}$ round bottom flask. After sparging the mixture with $\Nu_{2}$ , $5\mathrm{ml}$ of triethylamine was added rapidly while stirring under an $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere. The $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ nanocrystals precipitated within minutes, but all reaction mixtures were stirred for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . The suspended $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobdc) nanoparticles were immediately collected by centrifugation, the solid was redispersed in $250\mathrm{ml}$ of DMF, and the suspension was heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{h}$ . The centrifugation and DMF washing steps were repeated five times to remove unreacted ligand. The nanocrystals were then collected by centrifugation, redispersed in $250\mathrm{ml}$ of methanol, and the suspension was heated at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . The centrifugation and methanol washing steps were repeated five times to exchange all of the DMF for methanol, including those molecules coordinated to the metal sites. Full removal of DMF was confirmed by infrared spectroscopy. Nanocrystals were then stored in methanol until membrane casting, or dried under reduced pressure at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ before gas adsorption measurements.
Membrane casting and activation. To prevent agglomeration, nanocrystals were not dried before film casting and formation. Concentrations of $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobdc) in methanol were determined by sonicating a stock solution and reducing a $\mathrm{1-ml}$ aliquot to dryness to find the mass of activated nanocrystals, and resulting stock solutions were found to be ${\sim}50\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ . For $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobdc)/6FDA-DAM mixed-matrix membranes, an aliquot from the $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobdc) stock solution in methanol was taken and redispersed in $10\mathrm{ml}$ of dichloromethane. The nanocrystal suspension was then sonicated using a horn sonicator for $1\mathrm{min}$ , adding additional dichloromethane to maintain a total volume of $\mathrm{10ml}$ . 6FDA-DAM was then dissolved into the $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobdc) suspension and the mixture was sonicated for another 1 min. The mixture was cast onto a glass plate and the dichloromethane was allowed to evaporate over the course of ${\sim}24\mathrm{h}-\$ the resulting films were found to be $40{-}70\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thick. The freestanding film was then dried in a vacuum oven at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ to remove residual dichloromethane.
Gas adsorption measurements. Low-pressure gas adsorption data between 0 and 1.1 bar were measured using a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 instrument. Samples consisting of $50\mathrm{-}100\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobdc) powder, polymer film, or mixed-matrix film were loaded into a preweighed tube, and heated at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The mass of the activated sample was then used as the basis for the adsorption measurements. After an adsorption isotherm was measured, the sample was reactivated at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ before measuring a subsequent adsorption isotherm.
Imaging of mixed-matrix membranes and $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobdc) nanocrystals. For cross-sectional transmission electron microscopy (TEM) samples, the mixed-matrix membranes were imbedded in epoxy resin (Araldite 502, Electron Microscopy Sciences) and cured at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ . The sample was then cut into ${\sim}100\mathrm{-nm}$ -thick sections using an RMC MT-X Ultramicrotome (Boeckeler Instruments) and collected on copper TEM grids. TEM images were obtained on a JEOL 1200 EX TEM instrument operating at an accelerating voltage of $120\mathrm{kV}.$ Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were collected at $5\mathrm{keV}/12\upmu\mathrm{A}$ using a field emission SEM instrument (JEOL FSM6430). Membrane cross-sections were exposed by fracturing in liquid nitrogen. For imaging $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobdc) powders, SEM samples were prepared by dispersing the nanocrystals in dichloromethane and drop casting onto a silicon chip. To dissipate charge, the samples were sputter coated with $\sim3\mathrm{nm}$ of Au (Denton Vacuum, LLC). TEM samples of $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobdc) powders were prepared by dispersing nanocrystals into methanol and drop casting onto copper TEM grids.
Dynamic light scattering measurements. Number-averaged particle size distributions were collected by dynamic light scattering. After synthesis, including washing in methanol, a 1-mg sample of the $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ nanocrystals was suspended in $20\mathrm{ml}$ of methanol and sonicated using a horn sonicator for $2\mathrm{min}$ . Measurements were performed at $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ on a Brookhaven BI-200sm instrument, using samples suspended in methanol and assuming a refractive index of 1.7 for the $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobdc) nanocrystals.
Calculating permeability. To ensure steady-state permeation rates are attained, permeability measurements were run for at least six times the time lag, where the time lag is defined as the intercept on the time-axis on the pressure versus time plot where a line is drawn fitting the linear region30. $t=0$ corresponds to when the downstream volume is closed to vacuum and the gas is allowed to begin accumulating. At the end of six times the time lag, the slope of the line fitting the last $20\%$ of the data was used to determine the steady-state permeation rate. In the case that the time lag was not detectable, that is, for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeation, the permeation at each pressure point was allowed to proceed for $3\mathrm{min}$ .
The pressure-based permeability is calculated using equation (1), where $P$ is the permeability, l is the thickness of the film, $V_{\mathrm{cell}}$ is the volume downstream of the membrane where gas is allowed to accumulate during a permeation test, $A$ is the area of the membrane exposed to permeation, $P_{\mathrm{f}}$ is the upstream pressure, $R$ is the gas constant, $T$ is the temperature in kelvin, $(\mathrm{d}\boldsymbol{p}/\mathrm{d}t)_{\mathrm{SS}}$ is the steady-state permeation rate, and $(\mathrm{d}\boldsymbol{p}/\mathrm{d}t)_{\mathrm{leak}}$ is the leak rate. We report permeabilities in the unit of Barrer (1 Barrer $=10^{-10}(c\mathrm{m}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})\mathrm{cm})/(\mathrm{cm}^{2}s\mathrm{cmHg})\}$ .
$$
P=\frac{l V_{\mathrm{cell}}}{A p_{\mathrm{f}}R T}[(\mathrm{d}p/\mathrm{d}t)_{\mathrm{ss}}-(\mathrm{d}p/\mathrm{d}t)_{\mathrm{leak}}]
$$
Uncertainty in the permeability was propagated from uncertainty in the film thickness, film area, upstream pressure transducer, temperature and downstream volume.
Calculation of permeability based on upstream fugacity. The true driving force for the permeation of gas is the gradient in the chemical potential across the membrane. Normally, pressure is used as a proxy for the chemical potential and is valid in the limit of $P\to0$ . At higher pressures, however, the pressure no longer remains an adequate proxy for the driving force because non-ideal gas behaviour becomes significant. Instead, the gas fugacity is a more accurate measure of the imposed driving force for permeation.
The fugacity-based permeability can be calculated by
$$
P={\frac{l V_{\mathrm{cell}}}{A f_{\mathrm{f}}R T}}[(\mathrm{d}p/\mathrm{d}t)_{\mathrm{ss}}-(\mathrm{d}p/\mathrm{d}t)_{\mathrm{leak}}]
$$
where $f_{\mathrm{f}}$ is the fugacity of the feed.
Calculation of permeability parameters. To further elucidate the mechanism of increased selectivity and permeability, it was useful to determine the relevant parameters contributing to the permeability. Assuming that permeation follows the solution-diffusion model in these films,
$$
P=D S
$$
where $D$ is the diffusivity in $\mathsf{c m}^{2}\thinspace\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ and $s$ is the solubility in $(\mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})/(\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{mbar}))$ . The solubility is also related to the equilibrium adsorption isotherm,
$$
S{=}\frac{\rho n_{\mathrm{o}}}{p_{\mathrm{o}}}
$$
where $\rho$ is the density of the membrane in $\mathrm{gcm}^{-3}$ , $n_{\mathrm{o}}$ is the amount adsorbed in equilibrium with the feed pressure in $\mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , and measured from the adsorption isotherm, and $p_{\mathrm{o}}$ is the feed pressure in mbar. The density of the membrane was calculated using the crystallographic density of the framework33, the density of the polymer, and the mass loading of $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobdc) in the polymer as measured by TGA. The diffusivity can then by calculated using $D=P/S$ .
Alternatively, the diffusivity can be calculated using the time-lag method,
$$
D=\frac{l^{2}}{6\theta}
$$
Polymer synthesis. Polyimides were formed from $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bis-(3,4-dicarboxyphenyl) hexafluoropropane dianhydride (6FDA) and diaminomesitylene (DAM) using standard chemical imidization techniques35,36. The dianhydride, 6FDA
(P/N H0771, CAS $\#$ 1107-00-2), and diamine, DAM (P/N T1275, CAS # 3102-70-3), were purchased from TCI. Before use, 6FDA was purified once by vacuum sublimation and DAM was purified three times by vacuum sublimation. N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) was purchased from Spectrum Chemicals (P/N M1557) and vacuum distilled immediately before use. Triethylamine (P/N TX1200-5) and acetic anhydride (P/N 320102) were purchased from EMD and Sigma-Aldrich, respectively, and were used as received. A dry atmosphere was maintained within the reaction glassware by flowing house nitrogen through a Drierite column (W.A. Hammond Drierite) upstream of the reaction vessel. All glassware was attached to flowing, dry nitrogen before being flame dried.
To a three-neck flask equipped with overhead mechanical stirrer, $2.152{\mathrm{g}}$ $\mathrm{^{'14.33mmol}},$ ) of DAM was added and dissolved in $15\mathrm{ml}$ of NMP. Following dissolution of the diamine, the three-neck flask was cooled to $0^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using an ice bath before slowly adding $6.364\mathrm{g}$ $\mathrm{14.33mmol)}$ of 6FDA and an additional $15\mathrm{ml}$ of NMP. The sample was stirred for approximately $24\mathrm{h}$ to form a viscous poly(amic acid). Next, approximately $2\mathrm{ml}$ of triethylamine and $5.4\mathrm{ml}$ of acetic anhydride were added as the activating and dehydrating agents, respectively, for chemical imidization. The solution was diluted with an additional $6\mathrm{ml}$ of NMP and allowed to stir for a further $20\mathrm{h}$ to form the polyimide.
Following imidization, the viscous polymer solution was precipitated by pouring the reaction solution slowly into approximately 1 l of methanol (MeOH), which was stirring at intermediate speeds on a stir plate. The polymer fibres were rinsed thoroughly with MeOH, placed in a fresh batch of MeOH, and stirred to extract residual reaction solvent for approximately $18\mathrm{h}$ . Next, the fibres were again thoroughly rinsed in fresh MeOH before stirring the sample in fresh MeOH for a further $18\mathrm{h}$ . After a final filtration and drying step, the polymer fibres were heated at $225^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for approximately $24\mathrm{h}$ under vacuum. Following these rinsing and drying steps, no solvent was observed in the polymer film as determined from thermogravimetric analysis.
